(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "The six teaching shapes. Audience: the model learning to reproduce the reference, and a person learning why the reference looks the way it does.")

(chapter :number "00g" :library "shapes" :kind "meta-chapter"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :author "Alfred Borden"
  :axis "sensation"
  :read-order-hint "read after 00f-style.slat, before 01-core.slat"
  :prose
"# The six teaching shapes

Made by Alfred Borden.

## What a shape is

A **shape** is a way of turning one verb into a runnable lesson. The reference
is not a dictionary — it does not list a verb and stop. It takes each verb and
runs it through a fixed set of shapes, and each shape asks the verb a different
kind of question. Learn the six shapes and you can read any verb record in this
book; learn them well enough and you can WRITE one.

There are six shapes, numbered 0 through 5. Shape 0 is the identity of the verb —
its full grid. Shapes 1 through 5 are lessons built on top of that grid. Every
shape ends in code that RUNS. If a lesson's code does not run, the lesson is a
bug, not a fact (see 00-intro.slat §9, RULE 2).

| # | shape | the question it asks |
|---|-------|----------------------|
| 0 | verb-identity (the grid) | What IS this verb — in every discipline, at every scale? |
| 1 | run-and-explain | If I run this, what comes back, and WHY, step by step? |
| 2 | decompose-and-label | What are this program's parts, and what is each one's job? |
| 3 | build-up ladder | Can I climb from one line to a real program, rung by rung? |
| 4 | reversals | Can I go backward — from words to code, from broken to fixed? |
| 5 | comprehension vocabulary | Can I name the pieces — combiner, seed, predicate, pure, tail? |

The anchor verb for this chapter is `fold`. Its full canonical grid is in
33-higher-order.slat. Here we use it to make every shape concrete.

## Shape 0 — verb-identity: the 15-program grid

Shape 0 is the whole verb. Every verb in this reference carries EXACTLY **15
runnable programs**: **5 rows × 3 disciplines (eng · math · cs) = 15**. Not one
example, not seven — fifteen. A chapter of N verbs therefore holds ~15N runnable
programs (00-intro.slat §13a, THE KEY).

The five rows each do ONE job and never bleed into another (00-intro.slat §6):

- **Row 1 — HOW (composition).** The verb written well, on a 1/3/5 module
  ladder: one module, then three, then five. Base Scheme is free and does not
  count as a module.
- **Row 2 — WHEN (audit).** An operator's real problem: which verb, and why
  THIS one and not a sibling, defended under audit.
- **Row 3 — WHERE-ELSE (dimension).** The same SHAPE in three substrates — the
  verb's own domain, a metaphor, and the math/logic beneath.
- **Row 4 — WHY (proof).** An algebraic invariant, a runnable test that prints
  `#t`, an ∀-statement, and what would falsify it.
- **Row 5 — WITH-WHAT-ELSE (emergence).** Chain the verb with others and name
  what emerges — a counter, a checksum, a centroid.

Across the three disciplines the same row is written three ways: **eng** (an
operator's framing), **math** (the invariant or structure), **cs** (the
algorithm and its cost). Fifteen programs, all of them running. Here is `fold`,
row by row, discipline by discipline — the identity of the verb.

  ;; Row 1 · HOW (1/3/5 module ladder)
  ;;   A (1 module):  (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4))                 => 10
  ;;   B (3 modules): (fold cons (list) (list 1 2 3))           => (3 2 1)
  ;;   C (5 modules): a histogram folds a stateful table
  ;; Row 2 · WHEN (audit)  — sum a list of onset strengths for a meter
  ;;   eng:  (define strengths (list 1 4 9 2 5)) (fold + 0 strengths)   => 21
  ;;   math: fold is the catamorphism  (α × β → β) × β × [α] → β
  ;;   cs:   type-guard the input so a bad caller fails at the boundary
  ;; Row 3 · WHERE-ELSE (dimension) — collapse a sequence by threading an accumulator
  ;;   list / audio-meter integration / series summation — one shape, three costumes
  ;; Row 4 · WHY (proof)  — the empty list returns init unchanged
  ;;   (fold + 42 (list))                                       => 42
  ;;   ∀ f, init.  (fold f init '()) = init
  ;; Row 5 · WITH-WHAT-ELSE (emergence) — fold + a table = a counter; + a hash-step = a checksum
  ;;   (stream-checksum '(72 101 108 108 111))                  => 10418

The machine-readable grid for this chapter's anchor is carried below as the
Shape-0 record; the CANONICAL 15 for `fold` live on the verb itself in
33-higher-order.slat and are the source of truth.

## Shape 1 — run-and-explain

Predict, then run, then explain the run as a substitution trace. The lesson is
not the answer; it is watching the answer come to be. `fold cons` reverses a
list because each element is consed onto the FRONT of the growing accumulator:

  (fold cons '() '(1 2 3 4 5))
    step 1: (cons 1 '())        => (1)
    step 2: (cons 2 '(1))       => (2 1)
    step 3: (cons 3 '(2 1))     => (3 2 1)
    step 4: (cons 4 '(3 2 1))   => (4 3 2 1)
    step 5: (cons 5 '(4 3 2 1)) => (5 4 3 2 1)

The prediction ('the list, reversed') and the trace agree. The SRFI-1 argument
order — combiner receives (element accumulator) — is exactly why the trace runs
this way; get the order wrong and the explanation contradicts the run.

## Shape 2 — decompose-and-label

Take one program and name its parts, the way HtDP names a function's pieces:
signature, purpose, inventory, template. A `fold` that sums a list:

  signature:  (list-of number) -> number
  purpose:    add every element, seeded at 0
  inventory:  the combiner `+`, the seed `0`, the list `xs`
  template:   (fold <combiner> <seed> <list>)
  body:       (fold + 0 xs)

Naming the parts is what lets a reader swap one out on purpose: change the
combiner to `cons` and you have reverse; change the seed to `'()` and the type
changes with it. Decomposition is the grammar of substitution.

## Shape 3 — build-up ladder

Climb from one line to a real program, and make EVERY rung run. The classic
ladder is generate → filter → reduce:

  rung 1 (generate):  (iota 6)                          => (0 1 2 3 4 5)
  rung 2 (filter):    (filter odd? (iota 6))            => (1 3 5)
  rung 3 (reduce):    (fold + 0 (filter odd? (iota 6))) => 9

Each rung is a whole program that stands on its own, and each rung is the
previous rung wrapped in one more idea. Nobody has to hold the finished program
in their head; they build it one runnable step at a time.

## Shape 4 — reversals

Go backward. Two directions:

**Code ↔ description.** Given the words 'sum the squares of 1..5', write the
code — `(fold + 0 (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (iota 5 1)))` => 55 — and given the
code, recover the words. Running both directions proves you understand the map,
not just one side of it.

**Broken ↔ fixed.** The reference keeps code that SHOULD fail, because a failure
for the right reason is a lesson. Two live ones (see broken-examples/):

  (fold-left + 0 (list 1 2 3))   ;; => error: unbound symbol: fold-left
  ;; fold-left is R6RS/acc-first and deliberately UNBOUND in Motoi.
  ;; The fix: use SRFI-1 fold — (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3)) => 6.

  (filter 42 (iota 6))           ;; => error: not a function: 42
  ;; filter's first argument must be a predicate, not a number.
  ;; The fix: (filter even? (iota 6)) => (0 2 4).

A grader for this shape cannot say 'exit 0 = pass'. It must know each block's
EXPECTED outcome and assert on the error class, so a block that fails for the
WRONG reason is caught (see 10.1, the grader upgrade).

## Shape 5 — comprehension vocabulary

Name the pieces so the pieces can be talked about. Every fold has a small fixed
vocabulary, and once a reader owns the words they own the shape:

- **combiner** — the two-argument function threaded through (`+`, `cons`, a lambda).
- **seed** (init) — the starting accumulator; for `fold` it always participates.
- **predicate** — a one-argument test returning a boolean (`odd?`, `even?`);
  the heart of `filter`, never of `fold`.
- **pure** — no effect, same answer every time; `(fold + 0 xs)` is pure.
- **tail position** — where a good implementation loops without growing the
  stack; `fold` recurs in tail position, `fold-right` does not.

A reader who can point at `+` and say 'combiner', at `0` and say 'seed', at
`odd?` and say 'predicate', has stopped reading tokens and started reading shape.

## Why these six, and why they run

The shapes are not decoration. Each one is a different Bloom-level demand on the
same verb: Shape 0 remembers and understands, Shape 1 traces, Shape 2 analyzes,
Shape 3 applies and builds, Shape 4 evaluates and inverts, Shape 5 gives the
vocabulary that makes the other five sayable. Six angles on one verb is what
keeps the training data from locking onto a single phrasing.

And every one ends in code that runs. That is the whole discipline: a lesson you
can execute is a lesson the interpreter can grade, and a lesson the interpreter
can grade is a lesson we can trust at a million pairs."
)

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; Machine-readable shape records. Each carries runnable programs with their
;; expected outcome so the fold generator can extract and re-grade them. Axis is
;; `sensation` throughout — these are concrete, hands-on, runnable lessons.
;; Anchor verb: fold (33-higher-order.slat). Second voices: filter, iota.
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(shape
  :id "0" :ref "R1" :name "verb-identity"
  :axis "sensation"
  :job "The full 15-program grid: 5 rows × 3 disciplines. The identity of the verb."
  :anchor-verb "fold"
  :canonical-source "33-higher-order.slat"
  :note "The 15 canonical programs live on the verb itself; these are the same programs, extracted, each with its expected value for re-grading."
  :programs (
    (:row "1-composition" :discipline "cs" :sub "A" :modules 1
     :code "(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4))" :expect "10")
    (:row "1-composition" :discipline "cs" :sub "B" :modules 3
     :code "(fold cons (list) (list 1 2 3))" :expect "(3 2 1)")
    (:row "1-composition" :discipline "cs" :sub "C" :modules 5
     :code "(define (histogram lst)\n  (fold (lambda (x h)\n          (hash-table-update!/default h x (lambda (v) (+ v 1)) 0) h)\n        (make-hash-table) lst))\n(hash-table-ref (histogram (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'a 'b)) 'a)" :expect "3")
    (:row "2-audit" :discipline "eng"
     :code "(define strengths (list 1 4 9 2 5))\n(fold + 0 strengths)" :expect "21")
    (:row "2-audit" :discipline "math"
     :code "(define (catamorphism-check f init xs)\n  (equal? (fold f init xs)\n          (if (null? xs) init (fold f (f (car xs) init) (cdr xs)))))\n(catamorphism-check + 0 (list 1 2 3 4))" :expect "#t")
    (:row "2-audit" :discipline "cs"
     :code "(define (safe-fold f init xs) (if (list? xs) (fold f init xs) (error 'need-list)))\n(safe-fold + 0 (list 1 2 3))" :expect "6")
    (:row "3-dimension" :discipline "eng" :domain "higher-order list"
     :code "(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4))" :expect "10")
    (:row "3-dimension" :discipline "math" :domain "series summation"
     :code "(define (sum-to n) (fold + 0 (iota n)))\n(sum-to 5)" :expect "10")
    (:row "3-dimension" :discipline "cs" :domain "sequence collapse"
     :code "(fold cons '() (list 1 2 3 4 5))" :expect "(5 4 3 2 1)")
    (:row "4-proof" :discipline "eng"
     :code "(define result (fold + 42 (list)))\n(= result 42)" :expect "#t")
    (:row "4-proof" :discipline "math" :invariant "∀ f, init. (fold f init '()) = init"
     :code "(define (empty-is-init? init) (equal? (fold + init '()) init))\n(and (empty-is-init? 0) (empty-is-init? 42))" :expect "#t")
    (:row "4-proof" :discipline "cs"
     :code "(equal? (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3)) (+ 1 2 3))" :expect "#t")
    (:row "5-emergence" :discipline "eng" :emergent "frequency counter"
     :code "(define (histogram lst)\n  (fold (lambda (x h)\n          (hash-table-update!/default h x (lambda (v) (+ v 1)) 0) h)\n        (make-hash-table) lst))\n(hash-table-ref (histogram (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'a 'b)) 'b)" :expect "2")
    (:row "5-emergence" :discipline "math" :emergent "argmax"
     :code "(define (max-by f xs)\n  (fold (lambda (x best) (if (or (null? best) (> (f x) (f best))) x best)) '() xs))\n(max-by (lambda (x) (* x x)) (list 3 -5 2 -1 4))" :expect "-5")
    (:row "5-emergence" :discipline "cs" :emergent "rolling checksum"
     :code "(define (stream-checksum bytes)\n  (fold (lambda (b acc) (modulo (+ (* acc 31) b) 65536)) 0 bytes))\n(stream-checksum (list 72 101 108 108 111))" :expect "10418"))
)

(shape
  :id "1" :ref "R2" :name "run-and-explain"
  :axis "sensation"
  :job "Predict the result, run it, then explain the run as a substitution trace."
  :anchor-verb "fold"
  :programs (
    (:predict "the list, reversed"
     :code "(fold cons '() (list 1 2 3 4 5))" :expect "(5 4 3 2 1)"
     :trace "(cons 1 '())=>(1); (cons 2 '(1))=>(2 1); (cons 3 '(2 1))=>(3 2 1); (cons 4 ...)=>(4 3 2 1); (cons 5 ...)=>(5 4 3 2 1)"
     :note "Each element conses onto the FRONT of the accumulator, so the list comes out reversed. SRFI-1 order (element accumulator) is what makes the trace run this way.")
    (:predict "the sum 1+2+3+4 = 10"
     :code "(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4))" :expect "10"
     :trace "(+ 1 0)=>1; (+ 2 1)=>3; (+ 3 3)=>6; (+ 4 6)=>10"
     :note "With a commutative combiner the reversal does not show; the running total climbs the same either way."))
)

(shape
  :id "2" :ref "R3" :name "decompose-and-label"
  :axis "sensation"
  :job "Name the parts of one program: signature, purpose, inventory, template (HtDP)."
  :anchor-verb "fold"
  :programs (
    (:code "(define (sum xs) (fold + 0 xs))\n(sum (list 1 2 3 4))" :expect "10"
     :signature "(list-of number) -> number"
     :purpose "add every element, seeded at 0"
     :inventory "combiner +, seed 0, list xs"
     :template "(fold <combiner> <seed> <list>)"
     :note "Swap the combiner to cons and it becomes reverse; swap the seed to '() and the return type changes with it. The labels are what make the swap deliberate.")
    (:code "(define (reverse* xs) (fold cons '() xs))\n(reverse* (list 1 2 3))" :expect "(3 2 1)"
     :signature "(list-of a) -> (list-of a)"
     :purpose "rebuild the list front-to-back, reversing it"
     :inventory "combiner cons, seed '(), list xs"
     :template "(fold <combiner> <seed> <list>)"
     :note "Same template as sum; only the combiner and seed differ. That is the whole lesson of decomposition: same skeleton, different organs."))
)

(shape
  :id "3" :ref "R4" :name "build-up-ladder"
  :axis "sensation"
  :job "Climb from one line to a real program, every rung a whole runnable program."
  :anchor-verb "fold" :second-voices ("iota" "filter")
  :programs (
    (:rung 1 :label "generate" :code "(iota 6)" :expect "(0 1 2 3 4 5)")
    (:rung 2 :label "filter"   :code "(filter odd? (iota 6))" :expect "(1 3 5)")
    (:rung 3 :label "reduce"   :code "(fold + 0 (filter odd? (iota 6)))" :expect "9")
    (:rung 4 :label "abstract" :code "(define (sum-odd n) (fold + 0 (filter odd? (iota n))))\n(sum-odd 10)" :expect "25"))
  :note "generate → filter → reduce → abstract. Each rung wraps the last in one more idea; each rung runs by itself."
)

(shape
  :id "4" :ref "R5" :name "reversals"
  :axis "sensation"
  :job "Go backward: words⇄code, and broken⇄fixed. Some blocks MUST fail."
  :anchor-verb "fold" :second-voices ("filter")
  :programs (
    (:direction "words->code" :prompt "sum the squares of 1..5"
     :code "(fold + 0 (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (iota 5 1)))" :expect "55"
     :note "1+4+9+16+25 = 55. Running it proves the map from words to code was faithful.")
    (:direction "broken->fixed" :broken "(fold-left + 0 (list 1 2 3))"
     :expect-error "unbound symbol: fold-left"
     :fixed "(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3))" :fixed-expect "6"
     :note "fold-left is R6RS acc-first and DELIBERATELY unbound in Motoi. The interpreter refusing it is a free correctness gate. The fix is SRFI-1 fold.")
    (:direction "broken->fixed" :broken "(filter 42 (iota 6))"
     :expect-error "not a function: 42"
     :fixed "(filter even? (iota 6))" :fixed-expect "(0 2 4)"
     :note "filter's first argument is a predicate, not a value. The error names the exact mistake."))
  :grader-note "This shape's grader must assert on expected outcome + error class, not on exit code alone (see 00-BIG-FOLD-PLAN §10.1)."
)

(shape
  :id "5" :ref "R6" :name "comprehension-vocabulary"
  :axis "sensation"
  :job "Name the pieces — combiner, seed, predicate, pure, tail-position — so the shape becomes sayable."
  :anchor-verb "fold" :second-voices ("filter" "fold-right")
  :vocabulary (
    (:term "combiner" :points-at "+" :in "(fold + 0 xs)" :gloss "the two-argument function threaded through the fold")
    (:term "seed" :points-at "0" :in "(fold + 0 xs)" :gloss "the starting accumulator; for fold it always participates (unlike reduce)")
    (:term "predicate" :points-at "odd?" :in "(filter odd? xs)" :gloss "a one-argument boolean test; the heart of filter, never of fold")
    (:term "pure" :points-at "(fold + 0 xs)" :gloss "no effect, same answer every time")
    (:term "tail-position" :points-at "fold" :gloss "where a good implementation loops without growing the stack; fold recurs in tail position, fold-right does not"))
  :programs (
    (:code "(fold + 0 (filter odd? (list 1 2 3 4 5)))" :expect "9"
     :label "combiner=+, seed=0, predicate=odd? — three named pieces in one line"))
)
